I thought that too, but it turns out that `IBuffer.data()` is a `cppwinrt` extension method [implemented directly as](https://github.com/microsoft/cppwinrt/blob/a2131900b9dc9a317ea041abc2927656246089dd/cppwinrt/code_writers.h#L1307):
```cpp
auto data() const
{
    uint8_t* data{};
    static_cast<D const&>(*this).template as<IBufferByteAccess>()->Buffer(&data);
    return data;
}
```
Meaning that the lifetime of the pointer ["is controlled by the lifetime of the buffer object."](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/cppcx/obtaining-pointers-to-data-buffers-c-cx?view=msvc-170#getpointertopixeldata)
With the way we initialized the buffer, it has a scoped lifetime which means we should be safe from leaks.

Now, I admit I have no idea how `Buffer` is implemented internally (closed-source...) so I can't say whether the underlying data is stack allocated or heap allocated. I imagine that the data is marshalled through and we are really only storing a pointer (COM pointer?) on the stack as it is -- meaning a scoped lifetime is probably just fine.
